From 452f5454c81a23794c8d624596cbb0493e72f8cf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Hooper <ryanh@openssl.foundation>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 13:17:20 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] DTLS 1.3: Fix simultaneous KeyUpdate deadlock

When both peers independently send their own KeyUpdate around the
same time, each one deferred acknowledging the other's KeyUpdate
until its own was acknowledged first. Since each side's ACK was
exactly what the other side was waiting for, neither side could
ever send it: a real deadlock.

RFC 9147 section 8, as corrected by errata 8047, has two separate
restrictions: don't use the new epoch's keys until our own
KeyUpdate is acknowledged, and don't send new post-handshake
messages until it is acknowledged. A bare ACK for the peer's
KeyUpdate is neither -- it is not new post-handshake content, and
it can go out under our current, still-valid keys as long as we
have not yet switched to the new ones.

Defer installing a DTLS 1.3 KeyUpdate's new write keys until its
own ACK arrives (dtls_process_ack() in ssl/statem/statem_dtls.c),
instead of switching immediately on send. Drop the hold-back guard
specifically at the KeyUpdate-acknowledgment transitions
(TLS_ST_SR_KEY_UPDATE / TLS_ST_CR_KEY_UPDATE), while leaving it in
place for genuinely new content (tickets, PHA responses).

Fix the resulting state-machine bug where the shared ACK-send tail
(TLS_ST_SW_ACK / TLS_ST_CW_ACK) reset hand_state to TLS_ST_OK after
sending this kind of ACK, which made SSL_read()/SSL_do_handshake()
incorrectly report the connection idle while our own KeyUpdate was
still outstanding; park at TLS_ST_SW_KEY_UPDATE / TLS_ST_CW_KEY_UPDATE
instead so the caller correctly sees SSL_ERROR_WANT_READ.

Also remove a redundant !SSL_IS_QUIC_HANDSHAKE(s) check in
ossl_statem_server13_read_transition(): it sat inside a block
already gated on SSL_CONNECTION_IS_DTLS13(s), and DTLS 1.3 and
QUIC are mutually exclusive, so the check could never be false
there.

Fixes: #32878
